    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study reports results about attention heads in a fine-tuned vision transformer, not neuron poles or any version's neuron-level measure. It discusses mechanistic interpretability but does not test or rely on the claim's neuron-level finding.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports head ablations and visual attention-map interpretations. These findings concern attention heads, a different unit from the neurons and neuron poles named in the claim, so the rows are linked to none of the listed versions. The paper's citations to other work are not recorded as results.",
    "test_reason": "The study inspects attention maps and ablates whole attention heads in one fine-tuned ViT regression model. It never measures individual neurons or neuron poles, so no version's neuron-level measure is tested. Its 'monosemantic/polysemantic' labels refer to heads, judged by visual inspection without counts.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Broadly correct. The authors conclude that ViT heads show functional specialization, distinguishing monosemantic heads (chirp-localizing, or early non-task feature detectors) from polysemantic ones. They also find that deeper heads matter more than early heads, which they say could be pruned.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses attention-map visualization and single-head ablation in a fine-tuned vision transformer; it does not audit neuron poles or test explanations on held-out activations.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The paper reports differences by layer: early-layer ablations have smaller MSE increases, and attention-map functions vary across layers and heads.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Visual inspection labels some ViT attention heads monosemantic: chirp-focused heads in layers 4 and 6–11, plus text, colour-bar, edge and corner detectors in layers 1–4. Others, e.g. L12H12, are labelled polysemantic. No counts are given.",
        "proposed_claim": "In a fine-tuned ViT, many attention heads show visually specialized, single-feature attention patterns.",
